---
// src/pages/blog/oeko-tex-certification.astro
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['oeko-tex-certification'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Understanding OEKO-TEX Certification in Intimate Apparel"
  description="What OEKO-TEX Standard 100 means for lingerie production, and why it matters for your brand's compliance and customer trust."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">OEKO-TEX Certification</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-oeko-tex.webp" alt="Close-up of tested fabric and adhesive detail on certified intimate apparel material" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">May 25, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Understanding OEKO-TEX Certification in Intimate Apparel</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        For intimate apparel — products worn closest to the skin — fabric safety is non-negotiable. 
        OEKO-TEX Standard 100 is one of the most recognized certifications globally, and here's why it matters for your brand.
      </p>

      <h2>What OEKO-TEX Standard 100 actually tests</h2>
      <p>OEKO-TEX Standard 100 tests the finished article for harmful substances - it tests materials, not the factory. The parameter list covers formaldehyde, heavy metals, azo dyes, chlorinated phenols, phthalates and the arylamines released when certain dyes break down.</p>
      <p>The part most buyers miss is that limit values change by product class, because the exposure route changes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Product Class I</strong> - articles for babies and toddlers under three. The strictest limits, because of skin sensitivity and mouthing.</li>
        <li><strong>Product Class II</strong> - articles worn next to the skin over a large area. This is where bras, underwear and shapewear sit.</li>
        <li><strong>Product Class III</strong> - articles used away from the skin.</li>
        <li><strong>Product Class IV</strong> - furnishing and decoration materials.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A certificate that says Standard 100 without naming the product class tells you very little. Class III on a bra is a certificate for the wrong product, and claims based on it do not survive a retailer audit.</p>

      <h2>Reading the certificate properly</h2>
      <p>Three fields decide whether the document is useful to you: the certificate number, the validity date, and the list of certified articles. That last one is where most claims fall apart.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A certificate covers <strong>named article categories</strong>, for example "ladies' underwear, knitted, dyed". A new lace you introduce next season is not automatically covered by it.</li>
        <li>It is issued to a <strong>specific company at a specific production site</strong>. A trading company holding a factory's certificate is not the same thing as the factory holding one.</li>
        <li>It <strong>expires annually</strong>. Ask for the current cycle rather than a PDF from three years ago.</li>
        <li>The certified scope lists <strong>components separately</strong> - fabric, lining, elastic, thread and any print or heat transfer. Your label supplier matters as much as your fabric mill.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The practical test takes two minutes: take the certificate number and look it up in the public OEKO-TEX buying guide yourself. That settles the question faster than any email exchange.</p>

      <h2>Where the certificate stops</h2>
      <p>Standard 100 certifies the material as tested. It does not certify that the factory keeps buying that material, and it does not cover chemicals introduced by your own decoration unless those were in the tested scope.</p>
      <p>Two things follow. First, ask for the certificate plus the mill's material declaration for the specific fabrics in your order. Second, put a clause in the purchase order requiring that any substitution of fabric, lace or elastic be disclosed and re-tested. Substitution is exactly where certified product quietly becomes uncertified product.</p>

      <h2>Why It Matters for Lingerie</h2>
      <p>Consumers are increasingly aware of chemical residues in clothing. OEKO-TEX certification is often required by European retailers and is becoming a baseline expectation in North America. For OEM/ODM production, OEKO-TEX certification also protects your brand from liability.</p>

      <h2>How to Verify</h2>
      <p>Genuine OEKO-TEX certificates have a unique ID and can be verified on the OEKO-TEX database. Always ask your manufacturer for their current certificate — they're typically valid for 12 months and must be renewed annually.</p>

      <h2>What to hold alongside it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>BSCI or Sedex</strong> - social compliance and worker welfare. A different question answered by a different audit.</li>
        <li><strong>REACH SVHC declaration</strong> - needed for EU sale regardless of OEKO-TEX status, and it is your own legal obligation as importer.</li>
        <li><strong>California Prop 65 assessment</strong> - relevant if you sell in the US and the product carries any print, coating or metal trim.</li>
        <li><strong>Detox / ZDHC conformance</strong> - increasingly requested by EU retailers as a supplement to Standard 100, focused on manufacturing input chemistry.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>None of these replace each other. A factory with excellent chemical testing and a poor social audit is still a supply chain risk, and the reverse is also true.</p>

      <h2>Common myths worth correcting</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"Standard 100 means the garment is organic."</strong> It does not. Fibre origin and chemical safety are separate questions, and organic content has its own certification chain.</li>
        <li><strong>"The factory is certified, so whatever it makes is certified."</strong> Only the named article categories are covered. New laces, new linings and new labels fall outside until added.</li>
        <li><strong>"It covers the whole supply chain."</strong> It covers the articles listed on the certificate, which the factory chose to submit. Dyeing and finishing subcontractors are covered only if they were in scope.</li>
        <li><strong>"It never expires if the product does not change."</strong> It renews annually regardless, because the limit values and the parameter list are themselves revised.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each of these is a claim a brand can put in marketing copy and later have to withdraw. Worth knowing
        before the copy is written rather than after.
      </p>

      <h2>BSCI vs OEKO-TEX vs ISO</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>BSCI:</strong> Social compliance — factory working conditions, wages, working hours</li>
        <li><strong>OEKO-TEX:</strong> Product safety — chemical testing of materials</li>
        <li><strong>ISO 9001:</strong> Quality management — production process consistency</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A well-equipped factory should hold all three. In practice most hold social and chemical certification and are weaker on quality management system documentation, which is the one you feel in month six rather than month one.</p>

      <h2>What certification costs, and who pays</h2>
      <p>Standard 100 is charged per application, by article category, and scales with the number of components in scope. A single-category intimate apparel application on existing mill material is modest. Adding a new lace, a new elastic supplier or a printed label expands the scope and the fee.</p>
      <p>This is why you should confirm the certified scope covers <em>your</em> bill of materials at quoting stage, not after sampling. A factory quoting a new seasonal fabric that falls outside their existing certificate is quoting you a compliance problem, whether or not they mention it.</p>

      <h2>What to ask for in writing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Current certificate number, product class and expiry date</li>
        <li>The certified article list, so you can check your BOM against it</li>
        <li>The issuing institute name, because Standard 100 is issued through authorised institutes and the name tells you who tested it</li>
        <li>A written commitment to re-test on any material substitution</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For how these documents fit into a supplier evaluation in general, see <a href="/blog/choosing-bra-manufacturer">how to choose a bra manufacturer</a>.</p>

    </div>

            <h2>A worked example from a real order</h2>
            <p>
              A brand develops a lace bralette in three colourways. The factory's Standard 100 certificate
              covers knitted ladies' underwear in the current cycle, product class II. Everything looks fine
              until the second season, when the brand switches the lace supplier to match a lower price.
            </p>
            <p>
              The new lace is not on the certified article list. The garment is now sold as OEKO-TEX certified
              while one of its components has never been tested in that scope. Nothing has physically changed
              about the product's safety profile, but the claim is no longer supported, and a European retailer
              auditing the supply chain will find it.
            </p>
            <p>
              The fix is procedural, not expensive: a purchase order clause requiring any material substitution
              to be disclosed, plus a re-test before shipment. It costs one conversation and a short delay, and
              it is the single most useful thing a buyer can add to a supplier agreement on this subject.
            </p>

            <h2>Certification is a floor, not a differentiator</h2>
            <p>
              Every serious factory in this category holds Standard 100 and a social audit. They are the entry
              ticket, not a reason to choose one supplier over another. What actually separates suppliers is
              whether they can hold a specification across a reorder, and no certificate covers that.
            </p>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Need an OEKO-TEX certified manufacturer?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">Charlizi Factory holds BSCI, OEKO-TEX Standard 100, and ISO 9001 certifications. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Contact us</a> for certification documents.</p>
    </div>
    <BlogSchema slug="oeko-tex-certification" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
